# Response to reviewers: acknowledges unresolved validation rather than suggesting all requests are fulfilled.
resp=Document();setup(resp)
head(resp,'Response to Reviewers — Submission 6222')
p(resp,'Original title: From Colonial Spatial Structures to Sovereign Spatial Planning: Multimodal Network Modelling and Optimal Location of New Urban Hubs in the Democratic Republic of the Congo Using Spatial Data Science.')
p(resp,'Revised title: '+title)
p(resp,'Author: PAMBOLO MAYAMBO Néhémie. Revision prepared: 6 October 2026.')
p(resp,'Dear Editor and Reviewers, thank you for identifying substantive gaps between the original framing and the implemented analysis. The manuscript has been rewritten as an exploratory analysis of existing-hub road coverage and model sensitivity. We have removed historical causal claims, unsupported travel-time estimates, and new-town recommendations; added complete gridded demand, an alternative population product, a directed mapped-road graph, exact optimization, and joint stress tests; and clarified the candidate-score and hydrological audits. We do not claim that all requests have been fulfilled. In particular, a compatible regional empirical validation dataset remains unavailable, and some proposed greenfield sensitivity analyses have not been performed. We respectfully ask that the revision be assessed in light of this explicitly reduced scope.')
responses=[
('1. Title, introduction, and the colonial-legacy claim','The reviewer requested either removal of the colonial claim from the title or a quantitative historical comparison.','Addressed by removal and scope correction. The title now identifies existing-hub road coverage and sensitivity. Section I states that no colonial-era network is digitized, and Section III.F explains why current coverage cannot apportion fragmentation between colonial inheritance and post-independence investment. Historical motivation is no longer a model result. No invented diachronic comparison is supplied.','Sections I, III.F, IV; revised title.'),
('2. MST + three-neighbour topology and centrality','The reviewer requested sensitivity to alternative graph topologies and distance thresholds, including Kenge and Bandundu.','New calculations compare MST + k for k = 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, and 8 and radius graphs at 15, 25, 40, 50, and 100 km. Kenge changes from betweenness rank 1 to 7 and Bandundu from 2 to 14 between k = 3 and 5. Radius 100 km yields 12 components and eight isolates. The centrality-based investment interpretation is withdrawn. The main model now uses mapped road geometry and preserves disconnection rather than forcing MST links. Closeness and reachability are exported for every node. Missing junction and restriction information remains a graph-quality limitation.','Sections II.C, II.F, III.B, III.D; Fig. 3; Table VII; topology_node_metrics.csv and topology_summary.csv.'),
('3. Sovereign hubs and functional polycentric planning','The reviewer asked how selected nodes can act as sovereign hubs and break dependency on Kinshasa.','The empirical model does not measure economic autonomy, employment, productive specialization, energy reliability, or trade dependence. These claims have therefore been removed from the title and results. Section III.E explains why Yanganga and Ebale are not validated sovereign hubs, while III.F distinguishes selecting several facilities from functional polycentricity. The main revised objective concerns existing candidates and population coverage. No added sovereignty score is presented as measured evidence.','Sections I, II.D, III.E–F, IV.'),
('4. Hydrological proxy specification','The reviewer requested the formula or GIS procedure behind high/moderate exposure.','The original cached rule is recovered and specified: high if distance to mapped water ≤5 km and slope ≤2%; otherwise moderate if distance ≤15 km and slope ≤5%; otherwise low, with missing inputs unclassified. The five-query SRTM90 kilometre-scale slope calculation is documented. We explicitly distinguish this heuristic from flood probability, depth, discharge, or a return-period map. The flag remains in the legacy audit and is not used to certify construction safety.','Section II.F; candidate_weight_audit_metadata.json; original environment script retained in the released package.'),
('5. Empirical validation using secondary observations','The reviewer requested validation against actual road, port, flight, field, or historical data.','Not fully addressed. The JICA Kinshasa April 2019 master-plan report was downloaded and its 2017 survey route inventory examined. It contains urban route observations, but these do not provide a compatible ten-province OD matrix or target for the regional 100-km coverage objective. No regional predictive error or field validation is reported. We removed the previous phrase “indirect validation”: source consistency, recovery of names, and robustness are now diagnostics and computational verification. This remains a substantive external-validity limitation; the author does not ask the editor to treat the calculations as observed mobility or validated operational planning.','Sections II.G, III.F, IV; reference [32]; jica_survey_route_inventory.csv. A matched empirical validation test is still required for stronger claims.'),
('6. Joint uncertainty rather than weights alone','The reviewer requested simultaneous uncertainty in population, OSM completeness, speeds, and coordinates.','Partially addressed by new joint calculations: two population products, one- and five-kilometre demand grids, main roads versus inclusion of tracks, connector limits 1/2.5/5 km, fixed 1% road-feature removal, and fixed 250-m hub-coordinate displacement. The full design has 96 configurations per p and 288 exact runs. For p = 10, coverage spans 40.40–63.35%. This envelope is not a confidence interval, and removal/displacement levels are uncalibrated stress assumptions. We withdrew travel-time claims, so no assumed speeds enter the revised objective. Road additions and distributions of positional or operational error are not calibrated.','Section II.E; Tables II and VI; joint_maximal_coverage_results.csv and joint_coverage_metadata.json.'),
('7. Justification of multicriteria weights','The reviewer requested empirical justification or expert elicitation for the criterion weights.','The manuscript now labels the weights as assumptions, not expert elicitation or AHP. The audit distinguishes the six preliminary weights from the actual seven-variable environmental Monte Carlo vector. Repeating 5,000 draws around uniform weights changes dominant winners in three provinces. Crucially, Yanganga is the only compatible cached candidate in Tshuapa, making its 100% selection frequency automatic. These findings remove the previous robustness endorsement. No unperformed consultation is claimed; greenfield recommendations are withdrawn.','Sections II.F and III.E; Table VIII; candidate_reference_uniform_weights.csv.'),
('8. Population alternatives, road completeness, thresholds, grid resolution, forest definition, and Sobol analysis','The reviewer specified WorldPop versus alternatives, segment perturbations, thresholds 15/25/40/100 km, grid resolution, forest definition, and possible Sobol decomposition.','Population product sensitivity uses actual downloaded GHS-POP. Topology radius tests use 15/25/40/50/100 km; main-road scenarios add/remove mapped features as described above. Demand aggregation is compared at 1 versus 5 km. These demand grids are distinct from the old 10-versus-25-km greenfield candidate-grid request, which has not been executed. Tree-cover definitions, protected-area masks, and tenure constraints are not newly tested. Because new-town recommendations are removed, the 75-point screen is a supplementary audit only. The factorial stress design is not labelled Sobol analysis and no variance decomposition is asserted. The stated omissions remain limitations rather than being presented as completed analyses.','Sections II.B, II.E–F, III.E–F; Table II; full supplementary run tables. Candidate-grid and ecological sensitivity remain unperformed.'),
('9. Complete population demand, standard urban definitions, and falsifiable elbow','The reviewer requested population cells or the full locality set, standard urban definitions, and a falsifiable hypothesis.','The principal model now includes every positive selected population cell: 219,279 WorldPop one-kilometre cells (24,553 five-kilometre cells) and 147,956 GHS-POP one-kilometre cells (21,120 five-kilometre cells). Rural and disconnected demand remain in the denominator. GHS-SMOD point classifications replace fixed-radius interpretations; they do not estimate full agglomeration extents. The old p-median is retained only as an audit, solved exactly for p = 2–20. A specified three-successive-relative-gains-below-5% rule identifies no elbow; the earlier ten-hub claim is withdrawn. The regional model changes to maximal covering because the mapped-road graph is disconnected.','Sections II.B, II.D, II.F, III.A, III.C–D; Tables III, V; Figs. 2, 4; elbow_rule_check.json.'),
]
